<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesc Macià</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesc Macià was a Spanish army officer turned Catalan nationalist who founded Estat Català and led the Republican Left of Catalonia. On 14 April 1931 he proclaimed a Catalan Republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, then agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> to accept autonomy within the Spanish Second Republic. He became the first President of the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Generalitat of Catalonia</a>, serving until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesc-macia.jpg" alt="Francesc Macià" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Espai Macià -Plaça Ramon Arqués, 5, 25400 Les Borges Blanques. Arxiu Josep Segura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macia_2a_tongada_scans_003_editora_8_44_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francesc Macià i Llussà was born on 21 September 1859 in Vilanova i la Geltrú, Catalonia. Soon after his father's death, when he was 16, he entered the Military Academy of Guadalajara to join the Corps of Engineers, specialising in bridges, railways and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>. He asked to be sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> but was posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, rising from telegrapher to captain and eventually lieutenant-colonel, and he favoured modernising the army.</p><p>In 1887 he was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a>, where he met his wife Eugènia Lamarca. In November 1905 army officers wrecked the offices of the satirical journal ¡Cu-Cut! and of La Veu de Catalunya. Macià publicly condemned this, and his superiors transferred him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santona">Santoña</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected for Solidaritat Catalana in April 1907, he was re-elected deputy in 1914, 1916, 1918, 1919, 1920 and 1923, and moved from Catalan regionalism towards left-wing independence. In 1919 he founded the Nationalist Democratic Federation, which proposed a federal or confederal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and in 1922 the independence party Estat Català. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera</a>'s coup in September 1923 he took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a>.</p><p>In 1926 he planned the Prats de Molló plot, an insurrection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> with 50 to 100 Italian mercenaries, which the French gendarmerie stopped. He was convicted and fined, but the failure made him popular in Catalonia. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> he founded the Casal Català there, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in April 1930 after a pardon and, after another brief exile, in February 1931.</p><p>In March 1931 Estat Català joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lluis-companys">Lluís Companys</a>'s Catalan Republican Party and the L'Opinió group to form the Republican Left of Catalonia, with Macià as leader. After local elections in April 1931 brought down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a>, he proclaimed the Catalan Republic from the balcony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a> de la Generalitat on 14 April, a few hours before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a> was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, hoping for an Iberian Confederation. Three ministers from Madrid reached agreement with him three days later, and the republic became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Generalitat of Catalonia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The provisional Generalitat drafted a statute of autonomy, which the Spanish Congress approved on 9 September 1932 after many changes. The Republican Left won a landslide in the first Catalan parliamentary election on 20 November 1932, and Macià was officially appointed President of the Generalitat, holding the post until his death. He died of appendicitis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> on 25 December 1933. His funeral drew a massive demonstration of grief, and he is buried in Montjuïc Cemetery. His papers are preserved in the National Archive of Catalonia and the library of the University of Barcelona. He achieved the first successful establishment of self-government for Catalonia in modern times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francesc Macià” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 14 April 1931 <b>Francesc Macià</b> of the Republican Left of Catalonia proclaimed a Catalan Republic in Barcelona.</blockquote><a href="/state/generalitat-of-catalonia-1931-1939">Generalitat of Catalonia (1931-1939)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vilanova i la Geltrú, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.22,1.73&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Vilanova i la Geltrú on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francesc Macià"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5111 4045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5111 4045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.22,1.73&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
